    "result_text": "The full build is green. There's a single test class (`AcceptanceTest`) with both new soft-delete tests passing, and `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\n## Summary\n\nI converted owner deletion to a soft-delete. `DELETE /api/owners/{id}` now flags the record instead of removing it, `GET` still returns the owner (with `deleted: true`), new owners get `deleted: false`, and the create endpoint's duplicate/identity checks skip deleted owners.\n\n**Changes made:**\n\n1. **`model/Owner.java`** \u2014 added a `deleted` (`Boolean`) field mapped to a `deleted` column, with getter/setter, following the existing nullable-`Boolean` flag pattern (`possibleDuplicate`, etc.).\n\n2. **DB schemas** (`db/{hsqldb,h2,mysql,postgres}/schema.sql`) \u2014 added a `deleted BOOLEAN` column to the `owners` table. The test profile is `hsqldb,spring-data-jpa` with `ddl-auto=none`, so the schema must be declared here; the others are updated for consistency.\n\n3. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `deleted` boolean property to the `Owner` response schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it and MapStruct auto-maps it into GET/POST responses.\n\n4. **`function/owner/BuildOwner.java`** \u2014 sets `deleted = false` on newly built owners, guaranteeing `false` (not null) in the create/GET response.\n\n5. **`function/owner/DeleteOwner.java`** \u2014 now `setDeleted(true)` + `save(owner)` instead of `delete(owner)`, retaining the row.\n\n6. **`function/owner/EnsureIdentityUnique.java`** (the 409 household duplicate check) and **`function/owner/FlagPossibleDuplicate.java`** (soft-duplicate flagging) \u2014 skip any owner flagged `deleted`, so a soft-deleted owner no longer blocks or flags a re-create.\n\nI scoped the \"ignore deleted\" filter to the duplicate/identity checks as the spec requires. This is safe against pre-existing behavior because no owner could be `deleted` before this change, so the filter is a no-op except in the new soft-delete scenarios. The JDBC repository impl was left untouched as it's off the test/default path (default and test profiles use `spring-data-jpa`).",
